     <article class="story single" data-beat="business">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">BUSINESS</span> · Economic Times · 2026-10-04 · editor 9/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Bezos-backed FireSat constellation to enable 20-minute wildfire detection</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Technology</span> <span>#Climate</span> <span>#FireSat</span></div>
       <p>The FireSat satellite constellation, supported by a $26 million investment from Amazon founder Jeff Bezos through the Bezos Earth Fund, has been launched to provide near real-time detection of wildfires globally. Developed by Muon Space for the Earth Fire Alliance, the program aims to identify fires as small as 15 feet by 15 feet within 20 minutes of ignition, a significant improvement over existing space-based monitoring systems that often detect blazes after they have caused extensive damage.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-04/bezos-backed-firesat-constellation-to-enable-20-minute-wildf.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The system's capabilities were validated during a March 2025 protoflight mission, where a demonstration satellite successfully detected a small roadside fire in Oregon that had been missed by other systems. By 2029, the constellation is expected to include over 20 satellites, providing hourly global revisits. Once fully operational in the early 2030s with approximately 50 satellites, the network will be capable of monitoring any point on Earth every 20 minutes or less. Lauren Sánchez Bezos, Vice Chair of the Bezos Earth Fund, noted that the technology is intended to help firefighters respond faster to prevent small flames from becoming major disasters.</p><p>Beyond immediate detection, the program aims to integrate satellite data into wildfire modeling and research to improve global management and resilience strategies. Projections indicate that the full constellation could reduce annual US land burned by 1.3 million acres and prevent 21.9 million tons of carbon emissions. Early adopters include fire organizations in the United States, Africa, Australia, and Portugal, with data access expected to expand to the broader research community by next year.</p><p>What to watch: The deployment of the full 50-satellite constellation by the early 2030s.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The report captures the core technical goals, investment figures, and strategic vision of the FireSat project.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/international/global-trends/us-news-bezos-earth-funds-26-million-firesat-could-detect-15-by-15-foot-fires-anywhere-on-earth-within-20-mins-lauren-snchez-bezos-says-i-watched-friends-and-family-lose-everything/articleshow/134638318.cms" rel="noopener">Economic Times</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
       <div class="sharebar" data-href="/article/2026-10-04/bezos-backed-firesat-constellation-to-enable-20-minute-wildf" data-title="Bezos-backed FireSat constellation to enable 20-minute wildfire detection"><b>Share:</b>
