     <article class="story single" data-beat="positive">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">POSITIVE</span> · The Better India · 2026-10-04 · editor 10/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Bihar Brothers Develop Magnetic Filtration for Arsenic-Tainted Water</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Bihar</span> <span>#water purification</span> <span>#arsenic</span> <span>#innovation</span></div>
       <p>Drinking water in parts of Bihar contained arsenic levels dangerously high, with WaterAid India reporting 704.4 ppb in Simri block in 2026. This figure is over 70 times the safe guideline of 10 ppb. Addressing this critical health issue, brothers Arpit and Abhijeet initiated a solution as a school project, according to The Better India.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-04/bihar-brothers-develop-magnetic-filtration-for-arsenic-taint.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>The insidious threat of arsenic is often invisible, being tasteless and odorless, and commonly found in shallow aquifers. Long-term exposure to this contaminant can lead to severe health problems, including skin lesions and various cancers. Durga Yadav, a resident of Bhikchu Ka Dera, tragically experienced this firsthand, losing her husband to stomach cancer and her brother-in-law this year. Despite knowing the water is poisonous, hand pumps remain her family's only option. The problem is widespread, with research detecting arsenic levels above 50 µg/L in 7,164 out of 66,623 samples across 50 blocks in 11 Bihar districts, putting over 10 million rural residents at risk.</p><p>Driven by this urgency, the brothers developed their innovative magnetic filtration technology at Bihar Bal Bhawan Kilkari. Their system specifically targets arsenic at the molecular level, utilizing magnetic separation instead of relying on conventional consumables or harsh chemicals. This approach offers a sustainable and potentially more accessible method for water purification.</p><p>After extensive testing, the technology transitioned from the classroom to real-world application. Field pilots conducted in Bhojpur and Buxar demonstrated remarkable success, reducing arsenic levels to below 2 ppb. This effectiveness was further validated by the Council of Scientific and Industrial Research (CSIR). The system has since been deployed in schools and communities, providing safe drinking water. Today, through their startup, Navmarg Research &amp; Innovation, Arpit and Abhijeet report that their systems have collectively delivered 3.19 million litres of arsenic-safe water across 13 different sites. For the brothers, this endeavor has always been about more than just building a machine; it is about providing a tangible alternative where safe drinking water is a necessity, not just a promise.</p><p>Why it matters: This innovation offers a sustainable solution to a critical public health crisis.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Excellent work integrating all statistical details and anecdotal context from the source text.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://thebetterindia.com/web-stories/innovation/bihar-arsenic-drinking-water-arpit-abhijeet-navmarg-filtration-innovation-12615360" rel="noopener">The Better India</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
