     <article class="story single" data-beat="cricket">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">CRICKET</span> · Crictracker · 2026-10-04 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Canada Super 60 Season 2 Day 3: Raza, Pooran, Ali Shine as Teams Secure Wins and Semi-Final Berths</h1>
       
       <p>On October 2, 2026, Day 3 of Canada Super 60 Season 2 at BC Place saw Mississauga Masters, Toronto Sixers, and Vancouver Anchors claim victories in the men's schedule, while Vancouver Warriors Women also secured a win. The day was highlighted by stellar performances from Sikandar Raza, Nicholas Pooran, and Moeen Ali, as the league phase progressed towards the semi-finals.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-04/canada-super-60-season-2-day-3-raza-pooran-ali-shine-as-team.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Mississauga Masters emerged victorious in the closest contest of the day, posting 125/2 before holding Montreal Royal Tigers to 123/4, winning by a narrow two-run margin. Sikandar Raza was central to their batting effort, reaching a half-century off just 24 balls. This result left Mississauga Masters unbeaten after their opening two men's matches in the league phase. Their strong performance continued into Day 5, where they finished the league stage unbeaten on 10 points, ultimately securing a semi-final berth, as reported by Crictracker.</p><p>Toronto Sixers successfully chased White Rock Warriors' target of 128/6, reaching 129/5 in 9.3 overs to win by five wickets with three balls remaining. White Rock's innings featured Aryan Sidhu's 40 from 16 balls, including five sixes, and Dominic Drakes' unbeaten 38 from just 10 deliveries with six sixes. Nicholas Pooran anchored Toronto's reply with an explosive 40 from 16 balls at a strike rate of 250, hitting five sixes. Kieron Pollard added 22 off nine balls, and Daniel Sams remained unbeaten on 27 off 10, including four sixes, also contributing with the ball by taking 2/26.</p><p>Vancouver Anchors were the first men's side to win on Day 3, bowling Brampton Blitz out for 84/9 in 10 overs before chasing down the target in 8.4 overs to finish on 90/4, winning by six wickets. Moeen Ali led their reply with an unbeaten 37 from 18 balls, striking one four and four sixes at a strike rate of 205.56, supported by Obus Pienaar's 22 from 13 balls. The bowling effort was equally impressive, with Lewis Gregory returning figures of 4/13 from two overs, while Martin Andersson and Akhil Kumar each claimed two wickets. Co-owned by Zaheer Khan, Vancouver Anchors also secured a semi-final berth on Day 4, as noted by Crictracker.</p><p>In the women's fixture, Vancouver Warriors Women beat Vancouver Anchors Women by eight wickets. Anchors Women posted 81/4 in 10 overs, with Fatima Sana Khan top-scoring on 49 from 31 balls, including five fours and three sixes. Sophie Reid then led the Warriors' chase with an unbeaten 37 from 16 balls, and Marizanne Kapp added 14 not out from seven, as Warriors reached 82/2 in 7.4 overs. Building on this, Toronto Sixers Women later clinched their second successive title, beating Vancouver Warriors Women by six wickets on Day 5, according to Crictracker.</p><p>Mississauga Masters and Vancouver Anchors have both qualified for the Canada Super 60 Season 2 men's semi-finals, with Mississauga finishing the league stage unbeaten.</p><p>What to watch: The upcoming men's semi-finals and the continued success of the Toronto Sixers Women.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: Comprehensive summary of all match results and league standings provided in the source material.</p>
