     <article class="story single" data-beat="world">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">WORLD</span> · The Hindu World · 2026-10-04 · editor 9/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Congo Ebola Death Toll Exceeds 4,000 Amidst Centre Burning and Worker Strikes</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Ebola</span> <span>#Congo</span> <span>#DRC</span> <span>#Public Health</span></div>
       <p>Congo's Ebola outbreak has recorded more than 4,000 deaths, with Health Ministry figures released on Thursday, October 1, 2026, showing 4,018 fatalities out of 8,300 confirmed cases. This makes it the fastest-growing Ebola outbreak in history and the second-most lethal on record, as well as the largest ever recorded in the country, according to Médecins Sans Frontieres (MSF). This grim milestone comes as a key Ebola transit and treatment centre in Kigonze, on the outskirts of Bunia, Ituri province, was burned down earlier this week by soldiers reportedly looking for weapons, forcing 19,000 people to flee.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-04/congo-ebola-death-toll-exceeds-4000-amidst-centre-burning-an.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Julien Harneis, the UN senior Ebola coordinator, highlighted the challenges, stating that creating such centres is a struggle due to shortages of money and staff. He further emphasized the critical impact of losing facilities, noting, &quot;When we lose a centre, that means we lose capacity, we lose investment, and we have to start again. And above all, it means that people do not get access to the care that they really need.&quot; The United Nations declared that &quot;the enormous challenge of halting the spread of Ebola… just became even harder&quot; following the destruction. The outbreak, announced on May 15, is caused by the rare Bundibugyo virus, for which no known treatment or vaccine exists.</p><p>Compounding efforts, health workers have gone on strike multiple times, demanding owed wages; protesting surveillance team members in Bunia waved placards stating, &quot;No money, no data!&quot; and &quot;No payment for months.&quot; More than 50 health workers have died after contracting the virus, and an aid worker with MSF was recently evacuated to the Netherlands, transferred to a high-level isolation unit at Leiden University Medical Centre. While confirmed cases slowed from around 850 cases and 470 deaths a week in mid-July to an average of 490 cases and 230 deaths in September, the outbreak still saw a 33 percent jump in both confirmed cases and deaths through September.</p><p>Widespread mistrust of health workers and authorities significantly hampers containment efforts, with residents dismissing Ebola as a hoax. This resistance is exacerbated by deep distrust in the government, stemming from its handling of past outbreaks and chronic corruption. The World Health Organisation has raised alarms about the virus's rapid spread into new health zones, straining limited resources. Africa CDC noted a fall in newly confirmed cases, but attributed this potential decrease to insecurity and challenges in community cooperation. The severity of the situation was underscored by the attack and beating to death of Congolese politician Marie-Célestin Karondwa, a local official with Congo's ruling Union for Democracy and Social Progress party, at his home in Butembo, an Ebola hot spot in eastern North Kivu province, earlier this week. His house was set on fire afterwards, and he died at a hospital from his injuries. This is the Central African nation's 17th Ebola outbreak.</p><p>What to watch: International and local efforts face ongoing challenges in securing public trust and containing the 17th Ebola outbreak amid political tensions and resource shortages.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft effectively synthesizes the reported outbreak data, incident details, and worker strikes, maintaining accuracy relative to the source.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.thehindu.com/news/international/ebola-treatment-centre-was-burned-down-as-death-toll-in-congo-passes-4000/article71537063.ece" rel="noopener">The Hindu World</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
