     <article class="story single" data-beat="cricket">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">CRICKET</span> · Crictracker · 2026-10-04 · editor 6/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Rest of India Leads J&amp;K by 265 Runs After Dominant Day 3, Dogra's 75 Highlights Day 2</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Irani Cup</span> <span>#Cricket</span> <span>#Jammu &amp; Kashmir</span> <span>#Rest of India</span></div>
       <p>Rest of India has established a dominant position in the Irani Cup 2026, leading Jammu and Kashmir by 265 runs at the stumps on Day 3, according to Crictracker. This substantial advantage marks a significant turnaround from the end of Day 2, when Rest of India trailed Jammu and Kashmir by a mere 17 runs, as reported by JioStar. The high-stakes encounter, held at the Sher-i-Kashmir Stadium in Srinagar, has witnessed fluctuating fortunes, with both sides showcasing strong individual performances.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-04/rest-of-india-leads-jk-by-265-runs-after-dominant-day-3-dogr.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Day 2, played on Friday, October 2, saw Jammu and Kashmir's captain Paras Dogra lead from the front with a brilliant batting display. Dogra crafted a well-composed 75 runs from 143 balls, an innings highlighted by six fours and one six. He forged a crucial 101-run partnership with Abdul Samad, who himself contributed a valuable 57 runs, providing a strong platform for J&amp;K. Towards the latter stages of their first innings, Lone Nasir Muzaffar continued the momentum, adding an impressive 64 runs to strengthen the team's total.</p><p>Despite J&amp;K's batting efforts, Rest of India's bowlers made significant inroads on Day 2. Left-arm pacer Mukesh Choudhary delivered an impressive performance, exploiting the conditions with movement and swing to claim a superb five-wicket haul. His figures of 5/78 included the key wickets of Qamran Iqbal, Shubham Pundir, Shubham Khajuria, Dogra himself, and Kanhaiya Wadhawan, putting the opposition under pressure. Choudhary was ably supported by Akash Deep, who picked up three wickets for 43 runs, and Mukesh Kumar, who chipped in with two wickets for 84 runs. This combined effort from the pace attack ensured regular breakthroughs and restricted Jammu and Kashmir's progress.</p><p>The substantial lead of 265 runs, established by Rest of India by the close of Day 3, sets the stage for a compelling final day as Jammu and Kashmir faces a challenging task to recover in the Irani Cup fixture.</p><p>What to watch: The final day's play as J&amp;K attempts to chase a significant target.</p>
       
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.crictracker.com/cricket-news/irani-cup-2026-jammu-kashmir-vs-rest-of-india-highlights-review-day-2-9992/" rel="noopener">Crictracker</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
