     <article class="story single" data-beat="tech">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">TECH</span> · Engadget · 2026-10-04 · editor 10/10 · 2 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Tesla Introduces Emergency Drive-Away Feature for Superchargers in US</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Tech</span> <span>#Automotive</span> <span>#Safety Feature</span></div>
       <p>Tesla has introduced a new Emergency Drive Away feature designed to allow drivers to disengage their vehicles from a Supercharger and drive away even while still plugged in, specifically in emergency situations. This significant update follows an incident a couple of months prior, where a driver was shot inside a Tesla taxi while it was charging at an In-N-Out Burger location in Twin Falls, Idaho. The shooting prompted calls from Tesla owners for a quick escape system, even suggesting they would pay for it to prevent potential abuse.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-04/tesla-introduces-emergency-drive-away-feature-for-supercharg.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Under normal circumstances, to move a Tesla from Park to Drive after charging, the charge port must be unlocked. This typically involves using the vehicle's infotainment system, the Tesla app, or pressing a dedicated button on the charger handle, a process that can be problematic when immediate departure is necessary. The new Emergency Drive Away feature, announced by Tesla on X on October 1, 2026, includes a short demo showcasing its activation.</p><p>To engage the feature, a driver shifts into Drive while the charger remains attached to the car. The infotainment system then displays a notice, requiring the driver to hold the brake pedal and tap &quot;Activate&quot; on the screen to override the lock. A clear warning accompanies this action, stating that driving with the cable connected will cause damage to both the vehicle and the Supercharger station, and explicitly advises, &quot;Use only in an emergency.&quot;</p><p>Access to this critical safety feature requires an upgrade to the latest software. Currently, its functionality is limited to Superchargers exclusively within the United States, and it does not support pre-2021 Model S and X vehicles. Tesla has also addressed concerns regarding potential misuse. The company stated on X that deliberate abuse &quot;will invoke additional penalties,&quot; further clarifying that &quot;electrical and civil cost can be up to $25k&quot; for physical and monetary damages incurred from such misuse. However, it remains unclear whether Tesla will impose charges for activating the drive-away feature in verified emergency situations.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft accurately incorporates all key details, figures, and context provided in the source material.</p>
       <div class="provenance">
      <b>Source</b>
      <ul><li><a href="https://www.engadget.com/2275509/tesla-supercharger-emergency-drive-away-feature/" rel="noopener">Engadget</a></li></ul>
      <p>AI-generated and fact-checked against the original report; claims the gate cannot verify are held back.</p>
    </div>
       
