     <article class="story single" data-beat="cricket">
       <div class="meta"><span class="beat-tag">CRICKET</span> · Crictracker · 2026-10-04 · editor 10/10 · 1 min read <span class="badge ok">fact-checked</span></div>
       <h1>Yuzvendra Chahal Retires from First-Class Cricket to Focus on White-Ball Return</h1>
       <div class="tags"><span>#Cricket</span> <span>#Yuzvendra Chahal</span> <span>#Retirement</span> <span>#Sports</span></div>
       <p>Leg-spinner Yuzvendra Chahal announced his retirement from first-class cricket on Friday, October 2, 2026. The 36-year-old concludes a red-ball career that spanned 49 matches and yielded 148 wickets. Chahal played his final first-class fixture for Northamptonshire in the County Championship Division Two against Middlesex at Lord's, a match that finished on September 26, 2026, where he claimed two wickets.</p><img class="hero-art" src="/img/2026-10-04/yuzvendra-chahal-retires-from-first-class-cricket-to-focus-o.webp" width="1200" height="675" alt="" loading="eager" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async"><p>Reflecting on his career, Chahal noted in an Instagram post that while a Test cap remained an unfulfilled dream, he is content with his journey. His first-class statistics include six five-wicket hauls, with his best innings figures standing at 6/44. He expressed gratitude to the Haryana Cricket Association for their support throughout his career in the format. Chahal stated that he has always held a deep love for red-ball cricket, which taught him patience and resilience.</p><p>Despite stepping away from the long format, Chahal confirmed he is not retiring from white-ball cricket. He remains determined to earn a spot in the Indian ODI and T20 squads, citing that he still has the hunger to play for his country. Chahal has previously represented India in 152 white-ball matches, claiming 217 wickets, and was part of the squad that won the 2024 T20 World Cup. His last international appearance for India occurred in August 2023 against West Indies. He intends to dedicate his future efforts entirely to limited-overs cricket to add to his international record.</p><p>What to watch: Chahal's performance in upcoming white-ball domestic tournaments as he bids for a national team recall.</p>
       <p class="critique">Editor's note: The draft is a complete and accurate summary of the source material, covering the retirement announcement, career statistics, and future intentions.</p>
